#. type: Title =
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:2
#, no-wrap
msgid "Artillery & Machines"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:10
msgid ""
"This section covers cannons, stone and bolt throwing war engines. Both "
"artillery and machines share some rules in common, so we’ve included them "
"together to avoid taking up more space than necessary. Special artillery "
"units and machines will be described in particular armylists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:15
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Artillery* describes crewed weapons such as cannons,\n"
"Stone Throwers, Flame Cannons, etc. In most cases,\n"
"these are arranged into units called *batteries*; a typical\n"
"battery consisting of two individual weapons.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:19
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"*Machines* describes unusual devices such as Steam Tanks\n"
"and Gyrocopters. Each model is invariably a single unit\n"
"in its own right.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Content of: <svg><g><text><tspan>
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:22
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/game-values.adoc:26
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/images/movement/unable-to-form-a-battle-line.svg:1169
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/images/movement/unable-to-form-a-battle-line.svg:1184
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stands"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:29
msgid ""
"Artillery pieces are mounted on stands which include the weapon itself plus "
"any necessary crew. For our purposes, the two are inseparable. After all, an "
"artillery piece without its crew is useless and crewmen without a weapon are "
"an embarrassment."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:31
msgid "Machine models are based on a single stand."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:34
#, no-wrap
msgid "Artillery in combat"
msgstr ""

#.  See the Command phase p.13–17. removed as there's nothing particular to artillery here
#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:42
msgid ""
"Artillery cannot use initiative to charge. The role of artillery is to shoot "
"at the enemy from a distance, not to fight. A player can order artillery to "
"charge into combat if he wants, although this is rather a desperate thing to "
"do!"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:46
msgid ""
"Artillery is vulnerable in combat. An artillery unit that wins a round of "
"combat never pursues and if it retreats it is automatically destroyed."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:50
msgid ""
"Machines are more complex then artillery and some have their own rules for "
"fighting in combat, as described in particular army lists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:53
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/flying-units.adoc:117
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/flying-units-at-a-glance.adoc:25
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/movement.adoc:271
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/movement.adoc:907
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/movement-at-a-glance.adoc:30
#, no-wrap
msgid "Terrain"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:59
msgid ""
"As discussed in xref:movement.adoc#terrain[Movement – Terrain], artillery "
"cannot move into or over terrain features on the battlefield except for "
"hills and bridges. Any other type of terrain is impassable to artillery."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:63
msgid ""
"Except where noted, machines treat terrain in the same way as chariots. Some "
"machines have special rules of their own, as described in individual army "
"lists."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:66
#, no-wrap
msgid "Shooting overhead"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:73
msgid ""
"As we described in the Shooting section, stands cannot see through other "
"stands. Consequently, missile-armed troops cannot shoot through or over a "
"friendly stand to attack an enemy. Technically speaking, the stand’s ‘line "
"of sight’ is blocked and it is unable to fire."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:85
msgid ""
"Whilst this is perfectly true and satisfactory for the majority of troops, "
"when it comes to artillery fire it is felt that an exception is called for. "
"After all, is it not appropriate that cannons, stone throwing engines and "
"the like should lob their missiles over the heads of intervening troops onto "
"the enemy? Obviously, it is of great benefit to crew if they can see where "
"their missiles land but we can conveniently assume that there are forward "
"observers placed in strategic positions to observe the fall of shot and "
"somehow communicate any targeting information back to the crew."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:101
msgid ""
"Artillery stands that are positioned on high ground can shoot over the top "
"of intervening troops on a lower level.  Artillery stands can shoot over the "
"top of intervening troops on the same or higher level if the target is on a "
"higher level still. In both cases intervening troops includes all friendly "
"units and any enemy units that are ineligible as targets – for example, "
"because they are engaged in combat. Enemy units that are eligible as targets "
"cannot be shot over because they have to be shot at – artillery being "
"obliged to shoot at the closest viable target in the same way as other "
"shooters. Eg, artillery placed on top of a hill can shoot over friendly "
"stands and combat engagements on lower ground. However, it cannot shoot over "
"intervening terrain such as woods, build-up areas and similar terrain "
"features that block line of sight."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:105
msgid ""
"Artillery placed on the ramparts of a castle or upper storey of a watch "
"tower is assumed to be higher than the corresponding ground level of the "
"fortress wall or tower."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:110
#, no-wrap
msgid "Cannons"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:120
msgid ""
"These are large calibre guns that fire a heavy cannon ball. Gunpowder "
"weapons like these are primitive and unreliable compared to the tried and "
"trusted technology of stone throwers and the like. Only Dwarf smiths and a "
"few weapon makers in the Empire can build these devices. These wild and "
"determined individuals can be recognised by their dense covering of soot and "
"lack of eyebrows."
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:126
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:219
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:261
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:296
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"|Range\n"
"2+|Attack\n"
"\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:126
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:261
#, no-wrap
msgid "3+.^|60cm"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:134
msgid "2+| 1/2 + bounce ^|↙ ^|↘ <|Close Combat >|Shooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:141
msgid ""
"Cannons ignore a target’s Armour because no armour can nullify a hit caused "
"by a cannon ball. No Armour roll is made. In addition, fortified targets "
"only count as being defended (5+ to hit) and defended targets count as being "
"in the open (4+ to hit)."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:152
msgid ""
"Cannon balls can bounce through densely packed ranks causing immense "
"destruction. To represent this, a shot is deemed to strike the closest point "
"of the target stand, pass all the way through it and bounce a further 5cm in "
"the same direction. If more than one cannon from the same unit is shooting "
"then both cannon balls are assumed to bounce the same distance through the "
"path between the closest cannon and closest target stand. The target unit "
"suffers two attacks plus +1 for each additional stand from the same unit "
"that is bounced through."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:161
msgid ""
"A shot that bounces into a different unit inflicts one attack on that unit "
"for each stand bounced through. This can result in units normally ineligible "
"as targets being hit, such as friendly units or units in combat. Total up "
"the number of attacks struck and roll for all the attacks against each unit "
"at a time."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:167
msgid ""
"If a cannon is charged it can shoot at the enemy by firing ‘grapeshot’. Like "
"cannon balls, grapeshot has an Attack value of 2 but it doesn’t bounce and "
"targets shot at by grapeshot count their full Armour value – armour is not "
"discounted as it is with a cannon ball."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:182
msgid ""
"Although grapeshot is pretty effective compared to, say, a shot from a "
"single archer stand, it is actually less effective than ordinary cannon "
"fire. At first glance this may seem wrong. After all, shouldn’t grapeshot be "
"devastating compared to normal cannon fire? The reason for this is not that "
"we’ve made grapeshot weak but that normal cannon fire represents not a "
"single shot but a continuous bombardment in which dozens of balls are fired "
"by each cannon. Grapeshot, on the other hand, represents just one hurried "
"blast at close quarters, far more deadly than a single cannon ball which "
"could easily sail over the enemy formation or pass between the ranks but not "
"as deadly as a rain of well aimed cannon balls over a longer period."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:201
msgid ""
"Because cannons can cause additional damage by bouncing shots, it is "
"possible to inflict hits upon friends or upon units from either side that "
"are engaged in combat.  Both of these are normally ineligible targets, but "
"in the case of bouncing cannon shots they can suffer hits as a result of "
"fire directed nearby. Any friendly unengaged units that take hits from "
"cannons are driven back as if taking enemy fire. Where friendly units are "
"driven back in this way wait until all drive backs for enemy units have been "
"completed, and then work out drive backs for friendly units. Any engaged "
"units taking hits from cannons will carry hits forward into the combat round "
"and hits count as having been struck during that round for purposes of "
"working out which side wins. Units engaged in combat are not driven back by "
"any hits taken – carrying hits forward takes this into account."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:204
#, no-wrap
msgid "Elven bolt thrower"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:213
msgid ""
"This elegant device was devised many years ago by the High Elves for use "
"onboard their ships. Since then it has become a feature of many Elven armies "
"and is sometimes known as the ‘Reaper’ because of its deadly effect. The "
"machine throws a hail of light weight bolts, ideal for raking the decks of "
"enemy ships or the ranks of enemy regiments."
msgstr ""

#. type: Block title
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:219
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:296
#, no-wrap
msgid "3+.^|40cm"
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:227
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:269
msgid "2+| 1/3 ^|↙ ^|↘ <|Close Combat >|Shooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:236
msgid ""
"The Elven Bolt Thrower can shoot at a charging enemy in the same way as a "
"missile-armed infantry or cavalry unit. Unlike many artillery pieces, the "
"Elven Bolt Thrower does not automatically pierce its target’s armour. "
"Enemies take their Armour rolls as normal. The Elven Bolt Thrower’s darts "
"are relatively small and light compared to say, the Undead Bone Thrower’s."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:241
#, no-wrap
msgid "Stone throwers"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:255
msgid ""
"Stone Throwers are big machines which employ a system of counterweights or "
"torsion power to chuck either one big rock, several small rocks or what (or "
"whoever) takes your fancy. These stone throwing machines are favoured by "
"greenskins because they are simple to build from readily available "
"materials. Because Stone Throwers lob rocks of such immense size no armour "
"can protect against them. Even the most heavily armoured knight would be "
"instantly pulped if a boulder the size of a horse were to land on him. To "
"represent this, targets always ignore their armour when shot at by a Stone "
"Thrower. No Armour roll is made."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:273
msgid ""
"Stone Throwers shoot with such a high trajectory that they cannot shoot at "
"charging enemies."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:281
msgid ""
"Because of the high trajectory Stone Throwers can shoot blind at targets "
"they cannot see. The targets are assumed to be reported by scouts or "
"messengers from other units.  If there is no target in sight a Stone Thrower "
"may instead shoot at the nearest enemy unit in range it cannot see.  When "
"shooting blind it always hits on 6+ no matter if the target is in the open, "
"defended or even fortified."
msgstr ""

#. type: Title ==
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:284
#, no-wrap
msgid "Bolt thrower"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:290
msgid ""
"A Bolt Thrower is a device fashioned to hurl large missiles upon the enemy. "
"It is essentially a big crossbow... a very big crossbow indeed! The missiles "
"it fires are as long as spears and can skewer a whole line of troops."
msgstr ""

#. type: Table
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:304
msgid "2+| 1/2 + skewer ^|↙ ^|↘ <|Close Combat >|Shooting"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:309
msgid ""
"Targets always ignore their armour when shot at by a Bolt Thrower. No Armour "
"roll is made. The Bolt Thrower’s heavy dart can pierce even the thickest "
"armour."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:316
msgid ""
"When you shoot with a Bolt Thrower it will strike up to two touching stands "
"within the missile’s line of flight.  These can be stands from the same or a "
"different unit – the missile will automatically pass through and strike up "
"to two stands so long as they are touching and along the missile’s path."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:323
msgid ""
"The target unit suffers two attacks plus +1 for [.edit]#an# additional stand "
"from the same unit within the missile’s path. A shot that skewers into a "
"different unit inflicts one attack on that unit. All attacks against the "
"same unit are rolled at once – for example, an infantry unit in column takes "
"three attacks so roll three dice."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:337
msgid ""
"Because a bolt thrower can cause hits on different units by skewering shots, "
"it is possible to inflict hits upon enemy units that are engaged in combat. "
"These are normally ineligible targets, but in the case of skewering shots "
"they can suffer hits as a result of fire directed at a unit touching another "
"enemy unit. Any hits on engaged units from either side will carry forward "
"into the combat round and count as having been struck during that round for "
"purposes of working out which side wins. Units engaged in combat are not "
"driven back by any hits taken – carrying hits forward takes this into "
"account. Units that are not engaged [.edit]#in combat# are driven back in "
"the usual way."
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#: en/revolution/modules/rules/pages/artillery-and-machines.adoc:341
msgid ""
"Bolt Throwers can shoot at a charging enemy and can do so at any point as "
"the enemy charges, including the start of the move or when the charger has "
"reached its final position."
msgstr ""
